        {type:"paragraph",title:"The Diwani",text:"After the Battle of Buxar in 1764, the Company became much stronger in eastern India. In 1765, Mughal emperor Shah Alam II granted the Company the Diwani of Bengal, Bihar and Orissa. Diwani meant the right to collect revenue. This gave the Company access to the resources of a wealthy agricultural region."},
        {type:"paragraph",title:"Why revenue was essential",text:"The Company was simultaneously a commercial organisation, a military power and an emerging territorial government. It needed money to pay soldiers, maintain forts, support officials, purchase goods and finance further expansion. Revenue from the countryside therefore became central to Company rule."},
        {type:"example",title:"Solved example — Why did Diwani matter?",question:"Why was obtaining Diwani more important than simply winning a battle?",answer:"A battle could give the Company temporary political advantage, but Diwani gave it a continuing source of income. Regular revenue could finance the Company's army and administration, helping it maintain and expand its power.",steps:["1. The Company obtained the right to collect revenue.","2. Revenue came from agricultural production and other local sources.","3. Money could be used for soldiers and administration.","4. A financially stronger Company could support further territorial expansion."]},
        {type:"important",title:"Important observation",text:"The countryside was not simply a background to political history. It became the financial foundation of colonial expansion."}
