        {type:"paragraph",title:"What are commercial crops?",text:"Commercial crops are crops grown primarily for sale rather than directly for household consumption. During Company rule, officials and European merchants increasingly encouraged the cultivation of crops that could be sold in Indian or overseas markets."},
        {type:"paragraph",title:"Examples",text:"Indigo, cotton, opium and other crops became commercially important in different regions and periods. Indigo was especially important because it produced a valuable blue dye used in textile production."},
        {type:"paragraph",title:"Why did the Company encourage them?",text:"Commercial crops could supply European industries and generate profits through trade. European demand influenced what crops merchants and planters wanted Indian cultivators to grow."},
        {type:"important",title:"Food versus cash",text:"A major problem arose when cultivators were pressured to use fertile land for commercial crops rather than food crops. The economic risks of commercial cultivation could fall heavily on cultivators."}
