        {type:"paragraph",title:"Why did rural people resist?",text:"Resistance could arise when taxes were too heavy, rents were oppressive, customary rights were threatened, crops were forced, debts became unbearable or local authorities abused their power."},
        {type:"paragraph",title:"Forms of resistance",text:"Peasants could refuse to pay, refuse to cultivate a particular crop, collectively refuse contracts, petition officials, appeal to landlords, attack symbols of authority or organise wider protests. The exact form depended on the region and issue."},
        {type:"paragraph",title:"Indigo as a major example",text:"The Indigo Revolt demonstrates how a crop-specific economic dispute could become a large collective movement. It also shows that peasants were not passive victims; they could organise, negotiate and resist."},
        {type:"important",title:"Historical thinking",text:"When studying a protest, always ask: What caused it? Who participated? What did they demand? What methods did they use? How did the government respond? What changed afterward?"}
